One pass tracking the largest and second-largest distinct values; skip values equal to the current largest. O(n) time, O(1) space. Sorting the distinct values is an acceptable O(n log n) approach.

def second_largest(arr):
    first = second = None
    for x in arr:
        if first is None or x > first:
            second = first; first = x
        elif x != first and (second is None or x > second):
            second = x
    return second if second is not None else -1

Two pointers from both ends; skip non-alphanumeric characters and compare in lowercase. O(n) time, O(1) space. Building a cleaned string and comparing with its reverse is also fine.

def is_palindrome(s):
    i, j = 0, len(s) - 1
    while i < j:
        if not s[i].isalnum(): i += 1
        elif not s[j].isalnum(): j -= 1
        else:
            if s[i].lower() != s[j].lower(): return False
            i += 1; j -= 1
    return True

Hash map from value to index; for each x, check whether (target - x) was already seen. Store AFTER checking so a value isn't paired with itself. O(n) time, O(n) space.

def two_sum(nums, target):
    seen = {}
    for i, x in enumerate(nums):
        if target - x in seen: return [seen[target - x], i]
        seen[x] = i
    return []

Push openers on a stack; each closer must match the top. The stack must be empty at the end. O(n) time, O(n) space.

def is_valid(s):
    pairs = {')':'(', ']':'[', '}':'{'}
    st = []
    for ch in s:
        if ch in '([{': st.append(ch)
        elif not st or st.pop() != pairs[ch]: return False
    return not st

Sort by start, then extend the last merged interval when the next starts at or before its end (take the max end). O(n log n).

def merge(intervals):
    intervals = sorted(intervals, key=lambda x: x[0])
    out = []
    for s, e in intervals:
        if out and s <= out[-1][1]: out[-1][1] = max(out[-1][1], e)
        else: out.append([s, e])
    return out

Kadane: at each element choose to extend the running sum or start fresh; track the best. O(n) time, O(1) space. Must handle all-negative arrays (don't reset best to 0).

def max_subarray(nums):
    best = cur = nums[0]
    for x in nums[1:]:
        cur = max(x, cur + x); best = max(best, cur)
    return best

Key each string by a signature that is identical for anagrams — its sorted characters (or a 26-letter frequency tuple) — and bucket strings by that key in a hash map. O(N·K log K) with sorting (K = word length).

def group(strs):
    from collections import defaultdict
    buckets = defaultdict(list)
    for w in strs: buckets[''.join(sorted(w))].append(w)
    return list(buckets.values())

Track the lowest price seen so far; for each day the best sale is today's price minus that minimum. O(n), O(1).

def max_profit(prices):
    low = float('inf'); best = 0
    for p in prices:
        low = min(low, p); best = max(best, p - low)
    return best

Keep a running sum of the previous three amounts; for each index from 3 on, flag it when amount > 5 * (sum/3), then slide the window. O(n).

def suspicious(tx):
    out = []
    if len(tx) < 4: return out
    s = sum(tx[:3])
    for i in range(3, len(tx)):
        if tx[i] > 5 * (s / 3): out.append(i)
        s += tx[i] - tx[i-3]
    return out

Track seen IDs in one set and already-reported IDs in another. On an ID already in 'seen' and not yet reported, append it. O(n).

def duplicates(requests):
    seen = set(); reported = set(); out = []
    for r in requests:
        if r in seen and r not in reported:
            out.append(r); reported.add(r)
        seen.add(r)
    return out

